# check-clean patterns — copy to `.check-clean.patterns` at the tree root
#
# This file exists because of a circularity: a guard that greps for your
# product's name has to contain your product's name, and the guard ships in
# the same repo it is protecting. The first version of check-clean.sh held
# the real terms inline, excluded `scripts/` from its own scan, and would
# have published the product name, the former product name, the staging
# hostname and the stack — inside the file whose whole job was keeping them
# out.
#
# So the terms live here instead, in a **dotfile at the tree root**. The
# publish procedure copies `./*`, and that glob does not match dotfiles, so
# this never leaves your machine. What ships is this example, with
# placeholders.
#
#     cp scripts/check-clean.patterns.example .check-clean.patterns
#     $EDITOR .check-clean.patterns
#
# Without it, check-clean.sh still runs — every generic rule works — but it
# cannot recognise *your* names, and it says so loudly on every run. That
# warning is the design: a guard that quietly stopped checking the most
# important thing is worse than one that never checked it.

# Every name that identifies you: the product, any former product name, the
# company, the people, the staging environment. Lowercase, `|`-separated,
# used as an extended regex. Be generous — a false positive costs one
# rewording and a miss costs a leak.
PRODUCT_TERMS='yourproduct|yourformername|yourcompany|yourstagingname'

# Infrastructure and vendor names that would identify your stack. These are
# separate from PRODUCT_TERMS so the failure message can tell you which kind
# of leak you have.
VENDOR_TERMS='examplehost|exampledb|examplequeue'

# Publishing under your own name means writing it down. check-clean.sh
# permits it in exactly two places — the copyright line of LICENCE, and the
# clone line of README — and nowhere else, in any other wording. Leave both
# empty if you are not publishing under a personal name.
AUTHOR='Your Name'
AUTHOR_HANDLE='YourGitHubHandle'

# Bare lowercase identifiers from your codebase that a hand-written example
# might carry over without anyone noticing: column names, table names, module
# names, internal field names. Rule 3 only catches `file.py:123` and rule 4
# only catches SCREAMING_SNAKE, so a bare column name in prose passes
# everything else. Leave empty if you have no worked examples drawn from real
# code; add to it the first time you de-identify one by hand.
IDENTIFIER_TERMS='your_real_column|your_real_table|your_real_module'
